# GraphQL Schema Generation & Workflow

## Overview

The project uses GraphQL as the API layer between the Symfony backend and Next.js frontend with automatic code generation.

## Schema Generation Process

### Full Workflow (Recommended)

```bash
make generate-schema
```

This command:

1. Validates GraphQL schema on backend (`php ./bin/console graphql:validate`)
2. Generates `schema.graphql` file (`php phing frontend-api-generate-graphql-schema`)
3. Copies schema to storefront
4. Generates TypeScript types and hooks (`pnpm run gql`)
5. Cleans up temporary files

### Manual Steps (Development)

```bash
# Backend: Generate schema
mutagen-compose exec php-fpm php phing frontend-api-generate-graphql-schema

# Frontend: Generate types
mutagen-compose exec storefront pnpm run gql
```

### Alternative Backend Schema Generation

You can also generate the GraphQL schema directly using:

```bash
mutagen-compose exec php-fpm php bin/console graphql:validate
mutagen-compose exec php-fpm php phing frontend-api-generate-graphql-schema
```

## File Structure

### Backend GraphQL

- **Schema Definition**: Defined in PHP resolvers and types
- **Generated Schema**: [project-base/app/schema.graphql](mdc:project-base/app/schema.graphql)
- **Resolvers**: Located in framework packages

### Frontend GraphQL

- **Query Files**: [project-base/storefront/graphql/requests/](mdc:project-base/storefront/graphql/requests/) - `.graphql` files
- **Generated Files**: Same directory with `.generated.tsx` extension
- **Schema Copy**: [project-base/storefront/schema.graphql](mdc:project-base/storefront/schema.graphql) (temporary)

## Writing GraphQL Queries

### Query File Example

Create a new file: `project-base/storefront/graphql/requests/products/productDetail.graphql`

```graphql
query ProductDetail($uuid: Uuid!) {
    product(uuid: $uuid) {
        __typename
        uuid
        name
        shortDescription
        description
        images {
            name
            url
        }
        price {
            priceWithVat
            priceWithoutVat
            vatAmount
        }
        availability {
            name
        }
        categories {
            name
            slug
        }
    }
}
```

### Using Generated Hooks

After running `make generate-schema`, use the generated hook:

```tsx
import { useProductDetailQuery } from 'graphql/requests/products/productDetail.generated';

const ProductDetail = ({ productUuid }: { productUuid: string }) => {
    const [{ data, fetching, error }] = useProductDetailQuery({
        variables: { uuid: productUuid },
    });

    if (fetching) return <div>Loading...</div>;
    if (error) return <div>Error: {error.message}</div>;
    if (!data?.product) return <div>Product not found</div>;

    return (
        <div className="space-y-4">
            <h1 className="text-2xl font-bold">{data.product.name}</h1>
            <p className="text-gray-600">{data.product.shortDescription}</p>
            <div className="text-xl font-semibold">{data.product.price.priceWithVat}</div>
        </div>
    );
};
```

## Development Workflow

1. **Modify Backend Schema**: Update resolvers or types in PHP
2. **Generate Schema**: Run `make generate-schema`
3. **Write Frontend Queries**: Create `.graphql` files
4. **Generate Types**: Schema generation creates TypeScript types
5. **Use in Components**: Import and use generated hooks

## Configuration Files

- [project-base/storefront/codegen-config.ts](mdc:project-base/storefront/codegen-config.ts) - GraphQL Code Generator configuration
- [project-base/storefront/schema-compress.js](mdc:project-base/storefront/schema-compress.js) - Schema optimization script

## Tips

- Always run `make generate-schema` after backend changes
- Use fragments for reusable field sets
- Generated hooks include TypeScript types automatically
- Schema validation prevents runtime errors
